// Manager v4 — 00006-unbalanced-zswap (EXPERIMENTAL_LANE / LANE-DEV-1)
// 00009 MEASUREMENT-ONLY NESTED ABLATION ARM `d23-shieldedkey-noop` — NOT A SHIPPING CONTRACT.
//
// This source is DELIBERATELY BROKEN. It exists only so that
//   weight(sub-component) = rows(w0-baseline) - rows(this arm)
// can be measured with `compactc --skip-zk` + `zkir-v3 mock-compile`.
// Ablated sub-component: every `shieldedKey` derivation (`persistentHash<Vector<3,Bytes<32>>>`) reached from `execute`
// It must never ship, never be promoted into contracts/manager.compact, never be
// proved, and never be deployed.
//
//
// ================================================================================================
// WHAT v4 ADDS TO v3, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT TOUCH
// ================================================================================================
//
// v4 = v3 + ONE SWAP-MAKER circuit. Every v3 circuit, guard, key domain and map layout below is
// BYTE-IDENTICAL to 00005's gate-green Manager v3 (`archive/00005/…`, base commit `e9701e9`), so
// 00005's whole negative suite still applies unchanged. Nothing was weakened, renamed or reordered.
//
// `openSwapShielded(colourA, valA, recipientA, coinB, creditAccount)` fuses a debit leg and a credit
// leg into ONE circuit, so a swap settles atomically BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by composing two
// calls, and `recipientA` — a `Maybe` — selects which FR-308 shape the offer takes:
//
//   recipientA = some(key)   FR-308 v1 (named taker) and v2(b) (bearer key).
//       `sendShielded` pays `valA` of `colourA` to that key and re-pools the change, so the A leg is
//       INTERNALLY BALANCED and the offer's only imbalance is the −B deficit a taker must fund. For
//       v2(b) the key is a throwaway whose secret ships in the offer envelope, so any holder can
//       settle and then sweep — that shape needs no contract support beyond this branch.
//
//   recipientA = none        FR-308 v2(a), the OPEN offer.
//       `valA` is released with NO OUTPUT AT ALL: the pooled coin is consumed as a zswap input, only
//       the change is written back, and the released value stands as a genuine POSITIVE imbalance at
//       the guaranteed segment, addressed to nobody. Any taker's stock balancer sweeps it, which is
//       what makes the offer usable by a holder whose keys the maker never knew (owner Q1, 2026-08-19).
//
// WHY ONE CIRCUIT AND NOT TWO — this is a MEASURED lane constraint, finding F-307, not a preference.
// An earlier v4 carried the two shapes as separate circuits, `openSwapShielded` and
// `openSwapShieldedSurplus`. It compiled, passed every offline test, and was then REFUSED ON DEPLOY
// 4/4 by the node with `1010: Invalid Transaction: Transaction would exhaust the block limits`. A
// deploy's dominant cost dimension is `bytesWritten`, which the contract's verifier keys dominate, and
// a bracket of probe contracts deployed on one stack located the ceiling exactly:
//
//     12 keys (v3)      27,791 bytesWritten   55.6% of the 50,000 ceiling   DEPLOYS
//     13 keys           30,070                60.1%                         DEPLOYS
//     14 keys           32,356                64.7%                         REFUSED
//
// So Manager v4's budget is THIRTEEN provable circuits: v3's twelve plus exactly one. The alternative
// was to delete v3 circuits to make room, which would have weakened the inherited contract — the one
// thing Plan 02 Phase 1 forbids. Merging is strictly better than that, and it is arguably better than
// two circuits anyway: the FR-308 shapes become an ARGUMENT rather than duplicated code, and they stay
// separately reportable because the argument, the resulting zswap structure and the settlement path
// are all distinct and separately measured.
//
// (The two pure helpers `zswapNullifierOf` / `zswapCommitmentOf` are free: they read no ledger state,
// so the compiler emits no proving key for them, exactly as for `shieldedKey`.)
//
// WHY THE SURPLUS CIRCUIT REIMPLEMENTS `coinNullifier` / `coinCommitment`: the pinned standard
// library keeps both PRIVATE (`standard-library.compact:252` and `:265` are unexported `circuit`s),
// and `sendShielded` — the only exported path to a zswap input — ALWAYS creates an output for the
// value it moves. Releasing value with no output therefore requires assembling the input, the
// nullifier claim and the self-change output directly. The reimplementations are byte-for-byte
// preimage-identical to the stdlib's and `manager.test.ts` proves the equality against the value the
// stdlib itself claims for the same coin, rather than trusting the transcription.
//
// FAMILY SCOPE (FR-310, owner Q3 → A): shielded only. The unshielded family gets NO swap circuit —
// a contract's unshielded payout is a UTXO in the maker's own bound intent, so its recipient cannot
// be left open, and the pinned wallet SDK's own unshielded swap test is `it.skip` (broken upstream:
// `FeeCalculation(OutsideTimeToDismiss)`). Unshielded is an EXTENDED GOAL for a follow-up project.
//
// ================================================================================================
// Manager v3 — 00005-open-colour-custody (EXPERIMENTAL_LANE / LANE-DEV-1)
//
// A FULLY OPEN custodian. Owner verdict on 00004's Manager v2, 2026-08-19: "I dont like we have to
// hard code the tokens. This will not actually work, as we don't know what or how many tokens colors
// the AA mananger will actually use." So v3 removes the colour list entirely.
//
// WHAT WAS REMOVED from v2 — and nothing else was loosened (FR-201):
//
//   - `configure(s1, s2, u1, u2)`                     gone. There is no admin circuit of any kind.
//   - `colourS1/S2/U1/U2`, `configured`               gone. No colour is knowable at deploy time.
//   - `assertConfiguredShielded/Unshielded(...)`      gone from every deposit, withdrawal and
//                                                     internal transfer.
//   - `registerAccount`'s seeding of four zero cells  gone. Registration creates NO balance cell.
//
// The Manager therefore deploys with ZERO colour information, and none can ever be registered. It is
// deployed BEFORE any Minter exists on its chain and custodies whatever arrives.
//
// WHAT DID NOT MOVE — the safety story is v2's, verbatim (FR-204, the critical requirement):
//
//   1. the witness choke point `authenticatedAccount()`, which derives the debited account from the
//      WITNESS and never from a caller-supplied parameter, and
//   2. the PER-(account, colour) guard, checked BEFORE any pool or contract-ledger guard, with a
//      MISSING CELL READ AS BALANCE 0.
//
// Opening admission does not open spending: `deposit*` may credit any REGISTERED account (credit is
// open — spec step 10 has OwnerM deposit into AA_A — while attribution still requires registration),
// and every debit still passes both guards above.
//
// --- lazy custody (FR-202) ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Custody state is created on FIRST CREDIT ONLY: a shielded deposit creates that colour's pool and
// the (account, colour) cell; an unshielded deposit creates the cell (the kernel maintains the
// contract's own unshielded balance); the CREDIT SIDE of an internal transfer creates the
// destination's cell. Nothing else creates anything. Every guard in every circuit is evaluated
// BEFORE the first write, so a refused operation is provably state-neutral — no cell, no pool, no
// map growth (NC-2, NC-3). A colour that is never credited is absent from every map and reads 0
// everywhere (FR-206).
//
// --- family-scoped storage (FR-203) -------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Shielded and unshielded custody live in STRUCTURALLY SEPARATE maps, and their composite keys are
// derived under DIFFERENT domain separators:
//
//   pools               Map<Bytes<32>, QualifiedShieldedCoinInfo>  colour -> pooled coin
//   shieldedBalances    Map<Bytes<32>, Uint<128>>   shieldedKey(account, colour)   -> amount
//   unshieldedBalances  Map<Bytes<32>, Uint<128>>   unshieldedKey(account, colour) -> amount
//
// Separate maps alone would already prevent aliasing; the separate key domains mean the two families
// could not alias even if the maps were ever merged. Probe P-COLL asserts this the hard way, with a
// MinterCollide deployment whose shielded and unshielded colours are BYTE-IDENTICAL by construction:
// the same 32 bytes must be tracked independently per family.
//
// This is also why v2's single colour-generic `transferInternal` is split into
// `transferInternalShielded` and `transferInternalUnshielded`. With byte-identical colours possible
// across families, a circuit taking only (to, colour, amount) could not say WHICH family it meant —
// exactly the ambiguity P-COLL exists to rule out. The spec's NC-5 and step 12 both name the
// shielded form.
//
// --- the invariant the harness asserts after every step (FR-205) --------------------------------
//
// Per colour c, over the DYNAMICALLY DISCOVERED colour set:
//     poolValue(c)                  == sum over accounts of shieldedBalances[shieldedKey(a, c)]
//     kernel unshielded balance (c) == sum over accounts of unshieldedBalances[unshieldedKey(a, c)]
// plus the EXACT size of all three maps. v2 could assert exactness because registration seeded
// `accounts x 4` cells; v3 seeds nothing, so exactness is now a statement about lazy creation
// itself: a map holds a cell if and only if some step credited it.
//
// Authorization is by witness. That is sound here because the Manager is always invoked in root
// position (00003: project 00002's non-root-witness blocker does not apply), and nothing here uses
// `kernel.caller()`.
//
// Derived from the pinned standard library at compactc-v0.33.0-rc.2 and from 00004's Manager v2.

pragma language_version >= 0.25.0;

import CompactStandardLibrary;

export {
  execute,
  depositShielded,
  depositUnshielded,
  zswapNullifierOf,
  zswapCommitmentOf,
  myAccount,
  isRegistered,
  accountRecord,
  shieldedAccountBalance,
  unshieldedAccountBalance,
  shieldedKey,
  unshieldedKey,
  poolValue,
  poolHasColour,
  evmAccountIdFor,
  evmDomainSeparatorFor,
  evmStructHashFor,
  evmDigestFor,
  semanticCommitmentFor,
  accounts,
  shieldedBalances,
  unshieldedBalances,
  pools,
  accountModes,
  evmOwners,
  evmNonces,
  deploymentDomain
}

/// The single fixed-width v1 action/auth envelope. Every inactive field is constrained to its
/// canonical zero representation before authentication or custody logic runs.
export struct ExecutePayload {
  selector: Uint<8>;
  authMode: Uint<8>;
  account: Bytes<32>;
  owner: Bytes<20>;
  accountSalt: Bytes<32>;
  nonce: Uint<64>;
  validUntil: Uint<64>;
  primaryColor: Bytes<32>;
  primaryAmount: Uint<128>;
  recipientKind: Uint<8>;
  recipient: Bytes<32>;
  toAccount: Bytes<32>;
  wantNonce: Bytes<32>;
  wantColor: Bytes<32>;
  wantAmount: Uint<128>;
  creditAccount: Bytes<32>;
}

export struct AccountRecord {
  registered: Boolean;
  mode: Uint<8>;
  owner: Bytes<20>;
  nextNonce: Uint<64>;
}

// --- ledger state -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Note what is NOT here: no colour cells, no `configured` flag, no admin key. The whole of this
// contract's colour knowledge is whatever these maps have accumulated from credits.

/// Pooled shielded custody, ONE merged coin per shielded colour, created on first credit.
export ledger pools: Map<Bytes<32>, QualifiedShieldedCoinInfo>;

/// Registered accounts. Registration publishes only a commitment to the owner's secret.
export ledger accounts: Set<Bytes<32>>;

/// Internal ownership attribution, per (account, colour), PER FAMILY (FR-203).
export ledger shieldedBalances: Map<Bytes<32>, Uint<128>>;
export ledger unshieldedBalances: Map<Bytes<32>, Uint<128>>;

/// Authorization policy is mode-separated. Native records have no EVM owner or nonce entries.
/// 0 = native witness; 1 = EVM EOA.
export ledger accountModes: Map<Bytes<32>, Uint<8>>;
export ledger evmOwners: Map<Bytes<32>, Bytes<20>>;
export ledger evmNonces: Map<Bytes<32>, Uint<64>>;

/// Fresh-deployment EIP-712 salt. No circuit writes it after construction.
export ledger deploymentDomain: Bytes<32>;

constructor(deploymentDomain_: Bytes<32>) {
  const domain = disclose(deploymentDomain_);
  assert(domain != default<Bytes<32>>, "deployment domain must be nonzero");
  deploymentDomain = domain;
}

// --- owner authorization --------------------------------------------------------------------

witness localOwnerSecret(): Bytes<32>;

/// Account identity = a commitment to the owner's secret. Registering an account publishes only the
/// commitment; spending requires the witness that opens it.
circuit ownerCommitment(sk: Bytes<32>): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentCommit<Bytes<21>>("aa00005:manager:owner", disclose(sk));
}

/// Assert the witness-derived account selected by `execute` is a registered native record.
/// THE single authorization choke point for every native debiting action (FR-204).
circuit authenticatedNativeAccount(acct: Bytes<32>): Bytes<32> {
  assert(accounts.member(acct), "caller's owner witness matches no registered account");
  assert(accountModes.member(acct), "registered account has no authorization mode");
  assert(accountModes.lookup(acct) == 0, "EVM account cannot enter native authorization");
  return acct;
}

// --- family-scoped keys -------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The two family tags are distinct CONSTANTS, so `shieldedKey(a, c)` and `unshieldedKey(a, c)` are
// different values for every (a, c) — including the byte-identical colours probe P-COLL mints.
//
// EXPORTED deliberately, though nothing on-chain needs them exported: they touch no ledger state, so
// the compiler emits no proving key for either (same as `myAccount`), and they let the harness
// derive the key for every cell BY RUNNING THE COMPILED ARTIFACT rather than reimplementing the
// hashing scheme off-chain. That is what makes the "exact map sizes, zero unaccounted keys"
// assertion an enumeration of real ledger state rather than a lookup of the cells the harness
// happened to think of.

circuit shieldedFamilyTag(): Bytes<32> {
  return pad(32, "aa00005:manager:shielded");
}

circuit unshieldedFamilyTag(): Bytes<32> {
  return pad(32, "aa00005:manager:unshielded");
}

export circuit shieldedKey(acct: Bytes<32>, colour: Bytes<32>): Bytes<32> {
  // 00009 d23 ABLATION: use the raw account as the map key instead of deriving one. Every CALL SITE is kept,
  // so Δ measures the key hashing alone — and, because this compiler does not
  // common-subexpression identical pure calls, it measures ALL of them.
  return acct;
}

export circuit unshieldedKey(acct: Bytes<32>, colour: Bytes<32>): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentHash<Vector<3, Bytes<32>>>([acct, colour, unshieldedFamilyTag()]);
}

/// A MISSING CELL READS AS 0 — never as an error, and never by creating the cell (FR-204, FR-206).
/// This is the read the per-account guard uses, so refusing an unbacked spend costs no state.
circuit shieldedBalanceOf(acct: Bytes<32>, colour: Bytes<32>): Uint<128> {
  const k = shieldedKey(acct, colour);
  return shieldedBalances.member(k) ? shieldedBalances.lookup(k) : 0;
}

circuit unshieldedBalanceOf(acct: Bytes<32>, colour: Bytes<32>): Uint<128> {
  const k = unshieldedKey(acct, colour);
  return unshieldedBalances.member(k) ? unshieldedBalances.lookup(k) : 0;
}

// --- setup ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Shared collision gateway. It seeds no custody cell and never stores a secret/signature/key.
circuit registerAccount(acct: Bytes<32>, mode: Uint<8>): [] {
  assert(acct != default<Bytes<32>>, "account id must be nonzero");
  assert(!accounts.member(acct), "account already registered");
  assert(!accountModes.member(acct), "account mode collision");
  accounts.insert(acct);
  accountModes.insert(acct, mode);
}

// --- AUTH-EIP712-AA-V3-V1 byte codec -----------------------------------------------------------

circuit reverseBytes32(value: Bytes<32>): Bytes<32> {
  return Bytes[value[31], value[30], value[29], value[28], value[27], value[26], value[25], value[24],
               value[23], value[22], value[21], value[20], value[19], value[18], value[17], value[16],
               value[15], value[14], value[13], value[12], value[11], value[10], value[9],  value[8],
               value[7],  value[6],  value[5],  value[4],  value[3],  value[2],  value[1],  value[0]];
}

circuit reverseBytes16(value: Bytes<16>): Bytes<16> {
  return Bytes[value[15], value[14], value[13], value[12], value[11], value[10], value[9], value[8],
               value[7], value[6], value[5], value[4], value[3], value[2], value[1], value[0]];
}

circuit bytes32LexicographicLt(leftValue: Bytes<32>, rightValue: Bytes<32>): Boolean {
  const leftHigh = reverseBytes16(slice<16>(leftValue, 0)) as Uint<128>;
  const rightHigh = reverseBytes16(slice<16>(rightValue, 0)) as Uint<128>;
  const leftLow = reverseBytes16(slice<16>(leftValue, 16)) as Uint<128>;
  const rightLow = reverseBytes16(slice<16>(rightValue, 16)) as Uint<128>;
  return leftHigh < rightHigh || (leftHigh == rightHigh && leftLow < rightLow);
}

circuit uint64Word(value: Uint<64>): Bytes<32> { return reverseBytes32(value as Bytes<32>); }
circuit uint128Word(value: Uint<128>): Bytes<32> { return reverseBytes32(value as Bytes<32>); }
circuit uint8Word(value: Uint<8>): Bytes<32> { return reverseBytes32(value as Bytes<32>); }

circuit addressWord(value: Bytes<20>): Bytes<32> {
  return slice<32>([...pad(12, ""), ...value], 0) as Bytes<32>;
}

circuit accountTag(): Bytes<32> {
  return Bytes[0x55, 0xbc, 0x94, 0x0f, 0x83, 0x53, 0x37, 0xf1,
               0x22, 0x4c, 0x18, 0x11, 0x10, 0xb2, 0xb7, 0x7f,
               0x57, 0xed, 0x69, 0x4c, 0xae, 0x0c, 0x4b, 0xf8,
               0xff, 0x6b, 0xb3, 0xe0, 0x3b, 0xe6, 0xa9, 0x88];
}

circuit domainType(): Bytes<32> {
  return Bytes[0x36, 0xc2, 0x5d, 0xe3, 0xe5, 0x41, 0xd5, 0xd9,
               0x70, 0xf6, 0x6e, 0x42, 0x10, 0xd7, 0x28, 0x72,
               0x12, 0x20, 0xff, 0xf5, 0xc0, 0x77, 0xcc, 0x6c,
               0xd0, 0x08, 0xb3, 0xa0, 0xc6, 0x2a, 0xda, 0xb7];
}

circuit domainName(): Bytes<32> {
  return Bytes[0xb2, 0xa1, 0x61, 0xc1, 0xe1, 0xfe, 0x09, 0xf6,
               0x31, 0x58, 0x5b, 0x3b, 0xda, 0x0e, 0x4a, 0x22,
               0xf3, 0x17, 0xd7, 0xc6, 0x63, 0xc5, 0x82, 0xa0,
               0x7c, 0x1d, 0x68, 0x3e, 0x61, 0xfd, 0xcd, 0xb1];
}

circuit domainVersion(): Bytes<32> {
  return Bytes[0xc8, 0x9e, 0xfd, 0xaa, 0x54, 0xc0, 0xf2, 0x0c,
               0x7a, 0xdf, 0x61, 0x28, 0x82, 0xdf, 0x09, 0x50,
               0xf5, 0xa9, 0x51, 0x63, 0x7e, 0x03, 0x07, 0xcd,
               0xcb, 0x4c, 0x67, 0x2f, 0x29, 0x8b, 0x8b, 0xc6];
}

circuit registerType(): Bytes<32> {
  return Bytes[0xe6, 0xac, 0xe6, 0xc7, 0x0a, 0x9d, 0x92, 0xef,
               0x85, 0x1c, 0x2e, 0x2a, 0x67, 0xb2, 0x30, 0x90,
               0x17, 0xb0, 0x51, 0xd3, 0x9e, 0x05, 0x54, 0xc7,
               0x46, 0x27, 0x4a, 0x17, 0x69, 0x59, 0xac, 0x4f];
}

circuit withdrawShieldedType(): Bytes<32> {
  return Bytes[0x71, 0x7e, 0x1e, 0x74, 0x12, 0x98, 0x52, 0xbd,
               0x43, 0x67, 0x44, 0xa5, 0xa1, 0x10, 0x8f, 0x0d,
               0xb9, 0x02, 0x92, 0x70, 0x31, 0xf5, 0xe7, 0x79,
               0x96, 0x18, 0xec, 0x12, 0x93, 0x66, 0xd6, 0x1e];
}

circuit withdrawUnshieldedType(): Bytes<32> {
  return Bytes[0xb6, 0x01, 0x29, 0xea, 0x6c, 0xa4, 0xc1, 0xb5,
               0x1d, 0x86, 0x60, 0x77, 0xd1, 0x1c, 0xdb, 0x02,
               0x30, 0xe6, 0x06, 0x58, 0x76, 0xa5, 0x42, 0x06,
               0xfe, 0xce, 0x04, 0x41, 0x3e, 0xda, 0xba, 0x9d];
}

circuit transferShieldedType(): Bytes<32> {
  return Bytes[0x06, 0xbe, 0xb8, 0x3e, 0xc8, 0xde, 0xd3, 0xa8,
               0x08, 0x0b, 0xfa, 0xb5, 0x91, 0xd8, 0x9a, 0x1b,
               0x86, 0xed, 0x9e, 0x3f, 0x8d, 0xf6, 0xc1, 0x0e,
               0xd3, 0x67, 0x74, 0x16, 0xd0, 0xa5, 0x60, 0x64];
}

circuit transferUnshieldedType(): Bytes<32> {
  return Bytes[0x46, 0xe9, 0x6f, 0x44, 0x96, 0xc1, 0x82, 0xe9,
               0x83, 0x95, 0xb6, 0x89, 0x70, 0x1a, 0x94, 0x5c,
               0xbd, 0xb4, 0x75, 0x43, 0x57, 0x42, 0x42, 0xcc,
               0x17, 0xf9, 0xb6, 0x44, 0x8c, 0x04, 0x9a, 0x07];
}

circuit openSwapType(): Bytes<32> {
  return Bytes[0xf7, 0x87, 0xd7, 0xf9, 0x63, 0xe8, 0x9e, 0xfc,
               0xda, 0x8e, 0x6a, 0x54, 0x6b, 0xaf, 0xff, 0x33,
               0x38, 0x8c, 0xbd, 0xf4, 0x4b, 0x81, 0xf6, 0xf5,
               0x95, 0x0c, 0x4b, 0xd3, 0xb0, 0x66, 0x58, 0x48];
}

circuit frozenTypeHash(selector: Uint<8>): Bytes<32> {
  if (selector == 1) { return registerType(); }
  if (selector == 2) { return withdrawShieldedType(); }
  if (selector == 3) { return withdrawUnshieldedType(); }
  if (selector == 4) { return transferShieldedType(); }
  if (selector == 5) { return transferUnshieldedType(); }
  if (selector == 6) { return openSwapType(); }
  return default<Bytes<32>>;
}

export circuit evmAccountIdFor(manager: Bytes<32>, owner: Bytes<20>, salt: Bytes<32>): Bytes<32> {
  const preimage: Bytes<128> = slice<128>([
    ...accountTag(), ...manager, ...addressWord(owner), ...salt
  ], 0) as Bytes<128>;
  return disclose(keccak256<Bytes<128>>(preimage));
}

export circuit evmDomainSeparatorFor(manager: Bytes<32>, domain: Bytes<32>): Bytes<32> {
  const alias = slice<20>(keccak256<Bytes<32>>(manager), 12);
  const preimage: Bytes<160> = slice<160>([
    ...domainType(), ...domainName(), ...domainVersion(), ...addressWord(alias), ...domain
  ], 0) as Bytes<160>;
  return disclose(keccak256<Bytes<160>>(preimage));
}

export circuit evmStructHashFor(manager: Bytes<32>, payload: ExecutePayload): Bytes<32> {
  const p = payload;
  if (p.selector == 1) {
    const preimage: Bytes<192> = slice<192>([
      ...registerType(), ...manager, ...p.account, ...addressWord(p.owner), ...p.accountSalt,
      ...uint64Word(p.validUntil)
    ], 0) as Bytes<192>;
    return disclose(keccak256<Bytes<192>>(preimage));
  }
  if (p.selector == 2 || p.selector == 3) {
    const typeHash = p.selector == 2 ? withdrawShieldedType() : withdrawUnshieldedType();
    const preimage: Bytes<320> = slice<320>([
      ...typeHash, ...manager, ...p.account, ...addressWord(p.owner), ...uint64Word(p.nonce),
      ...uint64Word(p.validUntil), ...p.primaryColor, ...uint128Word(p.primaryAmount),
      ...uint8Word(p.recipientKind), ...p.recipient
    ], 0) as Bytes<320>;
    return disclose(keccak256<Bytes<320>>(preimage));
  }
  if (p.selector == 4 || p.selector == 5) {
    const typeHash = p.selector == 4 ? transferShieldedType() : transferUnshieldedType();
    const preimage: Bytes<288> = slice<288>([
      ...typeHash, ...manager, ...p.account, ...addressWord(p.owner), ...uint64Word(p.nonce),
      ...uint64Word(p.validUntil), ...p.toAccount, ...p.primaryColor, ...uint128Word(p.primaryAmount)
    ], 0) as Bytes<288>;
    return disclose(keccak256<Bytes<288>>(preimage));
  }
  assert(p.selector == 6, "EIP-712 selector must be 1..6");
  const preimage: Bytes<448> = slice<448>([
    ...openSwapType(), ...manager, ...p.account, ...addressWord(p.owner), ...uint64Word(p.nonce),
    ...uint64Word(p.validUntil), ...p.primaryColor, ...uint128Word(p.primaryAmount),
    ...uint8Word(p.recipientKind), ...p.recipient, ...p.wantNonce, ...p.wantColor,
    ...uint128Word(p.wantAmount), ...p.creditAccount
  ], 0) as Bytes<448>;
  return disclose(keccak256<Bytes<448>>(preimage));
}

circuit eip712Digest(domain: Bytes<32>, structHash: Bytes<32>): Bytes<32> {
  const preimage: Bytes<66> = slice<66>([0x19, 0x01, ...domain, ...structHash], 0) as Bytes<66>;
  return keccak256<Bytes<66>>(preimage);
}

export circuit evmDigestFor(
  manager: Bytes<32>, domain: Bytes<32>, payload: ExecutePayload
): Bytes<32> {
  return disclose(eip712Digest(
    evmDomainSeparatorFor(manager, domain), evmStructHashFor(manager, payload)
  ));
}

// --- readers -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The account id (owner commitment) for the witness the caller supplies. Returns only the
/// commitment — never the secret — so it is safe to expose: the commitment is exactly what
/// `registerAccount` publishes.
export circuit myAccount(): Bytes<32> {
  return ownerCommitment(localOwnerSecret());
}

export circuit isRegistered(owner: Bytes<32>): Boolean {
  return accounts.member(disclose(owner));
}

/// One browser-facing policy query. Native records return zero owner/nonce without creating EVM
/// map entries; unknown accounts return the all-zero inactive record.
export circuit accountRecord(account: Bytes<32>): AccountRecord {
  const acct = disclose(account);
  if (!accounts.member(acct)) {
    return AccountRecord{
      registered: false,
      mode: 0,
      owner: default<Bytes<20>>,
      nextNonce: 0
    };
  }
  const mode = accountModes.lookup(acct);
  if (mode == 0) {
    assert(!evmOwners.member(acct) && !evmNonces.member(acct), "native record carries EVM state");
    return AccountRecord{
      registered: true,
      mode: 0,
      owner: default<Bytes<20>>,
      nextNonce: 0
    };
  }
  assert(mode == 1, "unknown account authorization mode");
  assert(evmOwners.member(acct) && evmNonces.member(acct), "EVM record is incomplete");
  return AccountRecord{
    registered: true,
    mode: 1,
    owner: evmOwners.lookup(acct),
    nextNonce: evmNonces.lookup(acct)
  };
}

/// One cell of the dynamic table, shielded family. Returns 0 for a colour this Manager has never
/// seen — that is the observable form of "missing cell reads 0" (FR-206).
export circuit shieldedAccountBalance(owner: Bytes<32>, colour: Bytes<32>): Uint<128> {
  return shieldedBalanceOf(disclose(owner), disclose(colour));
}

/// The same cell in the OTHER family. For a P-COLL colour these two reads take the same 32-byte
/// `colour` argument and must still answer independently.
export circuit unshieldedAccountBalance(owner: Bytes<32>, colour: Bytes<32>): Uint<128> {
  return unshieldedBalanceOf(disclose(owner), disclose(colour));
}

export circuit poolValue(colour: Bytes<32>): Uint<128> {
  const col = disclose(colour);
  return pools.member(col) ? pools.lookup(col).value : 0;
}

export circuit poolHasColour(colour: Bytes<32>): Boolean {
  return pools.member(disclose(colour));
}

// --- shielded custody ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Claim an incoming shielded coin and credit it to `account` under the coin's OWN colour.
///
/// THE OPEN PATH (FR-202, FR-205): there is no colour check. Whatever colour the coin carries is
/// custodied, including a colour minted after this Manager was deployed. The pool for that colour and
/// the (account, colour) cell are created HERE if they do not exist, and only here.
///
/// The stdlib only auto-receives when the recipient is this contract itself, so a transfer into the
/// Manager commits only if THIS circuit runs in the same transaction as the sender's operation.
export circuit depositShielded(coin: ShieldedCoinInfo, account: Bytes<32>): [] {
  // The deposited coin and target account are public facts of this transfer: the pooled coin and
  // the credited balance both land in ledger state, so they are disclosed once, up front.
  const c = disclose(coin);
  const acct = disclose(account);

  // Every guard precedes every write, so a refusal creates nothing (FR-202).
  assert(c.value > 0, "deposit must be positive");
  assert(accounts.member(acct), "credit account is not registered");

  // Must precede insertCoin: this is what allocates the Merkle-tree index.
  receiveShielded(c);

  if (pools.member(c.color)) {
    // Per-colour merge-on-deposit: each colour's pool stays exactly one coin.
    pools.insertCoin(
      c.color,
      mergeCoinImmediate(pools.lookup(c.color), c),
      right<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>(kernel.self())
    );
  } else {
    // FIRST CREDIT of this colour — the pool is created lazily, right here.
    pools.insertCoin(c.color, c, right<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>(kernel.self()));
  }

  shieldedBalances.insert(
    shieldedKey(acct, c.color),
    (shieldedBalanceOf(acct, c.color) + c.value) as Uint<128>
  );
}

/// Pay `value` of `colour` out of that colour's pool to `recipient`, debiting the WITNESS OWNER's
/// account. FR-204 guard order is load-bearing and is asserted by NC-1, NC-2 and NC-3.
circuit withdrawShielded(
  account: Bytes<32>,
  colour: Bytes<32>,
  value: Uint<128>,
  recipient: Either<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>
): ShieldedCoinInfo {
  const col = disclose(colour);
  const val = disclose(value);

  // 1. authorization already derived and checked the account before dispatch.
  const acct = account;
  // 2. THE PER-ACCOUNT, PER-COLOUR GUARD, BEFORE any pool guard (FR-204). A MISSING cell reads 0, so
  //    a colour this account has never held is refused here without creating a cell for it — that is
  //    NC-2 (pool rich, account absent) and NC-3 (colour dormant everywhere) in one line.
  assert(shieldedBalanceOf(acct, col) >= val, "account colour balance too low");
  // 3. only now, the pool.
  assert(pools.member(col), "no pooled coin for this colour");
  assert(pools.lookup(col).value >= val, "pooled colour balance too low");

  const result = sendShielded(pools.lookup(col), disclose(recipient), val);

  if (result.change.is_some) {
    pools.insertCoin(col, result.change.value, right<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>(kernel.self()));
  } else {
    // Fully spent: the colour leaves the map entirely.
    pools.remove(col);
  }

  shieldedBalances.insert(shieldedKey(acct, col), (shieldedBalanceOf(acct, col) - val) as Uint<128>);
  return result.sent;
}

// --- unshielded custody -------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Credit `amount` of `colour` to `account`, unshielded family.
///
/// No colour check, by design (FR-201). The LEDGER is what enforces honesty here: `receiveUnshielded`
/// only increments this contract's declared unshielded inputs, so a transaction that names a colour
/// it is not actually carrying fails to BALANCE and is refused by the node. The spec records that as
/// an assumption to be ASSERTED by G3 evidence, not assumed.
export circuit depositUnshielded(colour: Bytes<32>, amount: Uint<128>, account: Bytes<32>): [] {
  const col = disclose(colour);
  const amt = disclose(amount);
  const acct = disclose(account);

  assert(amt > 0, "deposit must be positive");
  assert(accounts.member(acct), "credit account is not registered");

  receiveUnshielded(col, amt);
  unshieldedBalances.insert(
    unshieldedKey(acct, col),
    (unshieldedBalanceOf(acct, col) + amt) as Uint<128>
  );
}

circuit withdrawUnshielded(
  account: Bytes<32>,
  colour: Bytes<32>,
  amount: Uint<128>,
  recipient: Either<ContractAddress, UserAddress>
): [] {
  const col = disclose(colour);
  const amt = disclose(amount);

  // Same order as the shielded side: witness, then the per-(account, colour) guard reading a missing
  // cell as 0, and only then the contract's own holdings.
  const acct = account;
  assert(unshieldedBalanceOf(acct, col) >= amt, "account colour balance too low");
  // Comparison guard rather than an exact-balance read (stdlib note on `unshieldedBalance`).
  assert(unshieldedBalanceGte(col, amt), "contract unshielded balance too low");

  sendUnshielded(col, amt, disclose(recipient));
  unshieldedBalances.insert(unshieldedKey(acct, col), (unshieldedBalanceOf(acct, col) - amt) as Uint<128>);
}

// --- internal ownership transfer -----------------------------------------------------------------

/// Move ownership of ONE SHIELDED colour between two accounts inside the Manager. NO token operation
/// occurs: that colour's pooled coin and the contract's ledger balances are byte-identical before and
/// after, and no OTHER colour is touched at all.
///
/// The CREDIT SIDE creates the destination's cell lazily if it does not exist (FR-202; spec step 12:
/// the (AA_B, S1) cell comes into being through an internal transfer, not a deposit).
circuit transferInternalShielded(
  account: Bytes<32>, to: Bytes<32>, colour: Bytes<32>, amount: Uint<128>
): [] {
  const dst = disclose(to);
  const col = disclose(colour);
  const amt = disclose(amount);

  const fromAcct = account;
  assert(accounts.member(dst), "destination account is not registered");
  assert(fromAcct != dst, "internal transfer to the same account");
  assert(amt > 0, "internal transfer must be positive");
  // The per-account, per-colour guard again — NC-5 exercises it with an account that is
  // demonstrably rich in OTHER colours, and it refuses BEFORE the destination cell is created.
  assert(shieldedBalanceOf(fromAcct, col) >= amt, "account colour balance too low");

  shieldedBalances.insert(shieldedKey(fromAcct, col), (shieldedBalanceOf(fromAcct, col) - amt) as Uint<128>);
  shieldedBalances.insert(shieldedKey(dst, col), (shieldedBalanceOf(dst, col) + amt) as Uint<128>);
}

/// The unshielded family's internal transfer. Same shape, different map and different key domain —
/// so the same 32-byte colour moved here can never touch a shielded cell (FR-203, P-COLL).
circuit transferInternalUnshielded(
  account: Bytes<32>, to: Bytes<32>, colour: Bytes<32>, amount: Uint<128>
): [] {
  const dst = disclose(to);
  const col = disclose(colour);
  const amt = disclose(amount);

  const fromAcct = account;
  assert(accounts.member(dst), "destination account is not registered");
  assert(fromAcct != dst, "internal transfer to the same account");
  assert(amt > 0, "internal transfer must be positive");
  assert(unshieldedBalanceOf(fromAcct, col) >= amt, "account colour balance too low");

  unshieldedBalances.insert(unshieldedKey(fromAcct, col), (unshieldedBalanceOf(fromAcct, col) - amt) as Uint<128>);
  unshieldedBalances.insert(unshieldedKey(dst, col), (unshieldedBalanceOf(dst, col) + amt) as Uint<128>);
}

// ================================================================================================
// v4 (00006-unbalanced-zswap) — THE SWAP MAKER
// ================================================================================================
//
// Everything above this line is Manager v3, unchanged. Everything below is additive.

// --- zswap primitives the pinned standard library keeps private ---------------------------------
//
// `coinCommitment` and `coinNullifier` are unexported circuits in the pinned standard library
// (`standard-library.compact:252` / `:265`). `sendShielded` is the only exported route to a zswap
// INPUT and it always creates an output for the value it moves — so an offer that releases value with
// NO output cannot be built out of exported pieces alone. These two circuits are transcriptions of the
// stdlib's, preimage-identical field for field: same struct shape (`Bytes<21>` separator, then the
// coin, then the recipient discriminant, then 32 bytes of recipient), same domain separators, same
// `persistentHash`. `persistentHash` digests the ALIGNED ENCODING of the fields, so the struct's NAME
// is irrelevant and only the field types and order matter.
//
// This is transcription, which is exactly the kind of code that is silently wrong, so the equality is
// PROVEN rather than asserted in a comment: `zswapNullifierOf` / `zswapCommitmentOf` below expose
// these values, and the unit suite compares them against the nullifier and commitment the STDLIB
// itself claims for the same coin inside `withdrawShielded`. If a separator or a field order were
// wrong, that test fails.

struct SwapCoinPreimage {
  // `midnight:zswap-cc[v1]` / `midnight:zswap-cn[v1]` — 21 bytes each, exactly as the stdlib.
  domain_sep: Bytes<21>,
  info: ShieldedCoinInfo,
  dataType: Boolean,
  data: Bytes<32>,
}

circuit zswapCoinCommitment(
  coin: ShieldedCoinInfo,
  recipient: Either<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>
): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentHash<SwapCoinPreimage>(
    SwapCoinPreimage {
      domain_sep: "midnight:zswap-cc[v1]",
      info: coin,
      dataType: recipient.is_left,
      data: recipient.is_left ? recipient.left.bytes : recipient.right.bytes,
    });
}

circuit zswapCoinNullifier(coin: ShieldedCoinInfo, addr: ContractAddress): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentHash<SwapCoinPreimage>(
    SwapCoinPreimage {
      domain_sep: "midnight:zswap-cn[v1]",
      info: coin,
      // The hypothetical `is_left` of `Either<ZswapCoinSecretKey, ContractAddress>` — a contract-owned
      // coin is always the RIGHT variant, so this is `false`, verbatim as the stdlib comments it.
      dataType: false,
      data: addr.bytes,
    });
}

/// The pooled coin without its Merkle index — the stdlib's private `downcastQualifiedCoin`.
circuit dropMerkleIndex(coin: QualifiedShieldedCoinInfo): ShieldedCoinInfo {
  return ShieldedCoinInfo{ nonce: coin.nonce, color: coin.color, value: coin.value };
}

/// EXPORTED so the equality above can be TESTED rather than believed. Pure: it reads no ledger state,
/// so the compiler emits no proving key for it (same as `shieldedKey`), and it discloses nothing — a
/// coin commitment and a coin nullifier are public objects that live in the transaction anyway.
export circuit zswapNullifierOf(coin: ShieldedCoinInfo, addr: ContractAddress): Bytes<32> {
  return zswapCoinNullifier(coin, addr);
}

export circuit zswapCommitmentOf(
  coin: ShieldedCoinInfo,
  recipient: Either<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>
): Bytes<32> {
  return zswapCoinCommitment(coin, recipient);
}

// --- the shared guard block --------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Both swap circuits open with the SAME guards in the SAME order, and the order is the owner-critical
// property carried verbatim from v3's `withdrawShielded` (FR-204, FR-305):
//
//   0. parameter sanity      — pure arithmetic on the caller's own arguments; reads nothing, leaks
//                              nothing, and cannot be confused with an authorization outcome.
//   1. THE WITNESS CHOKE POINT — the debited account is DERIVED from the witness, never supplied
//                              (NC-305: an unregistered witness dies here, before any balance is read).
//   2. THE PER-(ACCOUNT, COLOUR) GUARD, BEFORE any pool guard, with a MISSING CELL READ AS 0
//                              (NC-306: a pool rich in A but a maker cell that is empty or short dies
//                              here — it never reaches the pool guard).
//   3. the pool guards       — only now does the contract's own holding matter.
//   4. the credit target     — the account the wanted colour is attributed to must be registered.
//
// Every guard precedes every write in both circuits, so a refused offer build is provably
// state-neutral: no cell, no pool, no map growth (FR-202 discipline, FR-305).
//
// Guard 0's colour-distinctness assert is NOT decoration, it is finding F-305 made explicit. Two
// same-colour shielded credits cannot be assembled in one transaction at these pins: the second needs
// the first coin's Merkle index, which is only allocated when the first is really inserted on chain.
// A swap whose two legs were the same colour would hit that as an opaque
// `invalid index into sparse merkle tree` from inside the proving path; refusing it here fails CLOSED
// with a readable reason instead.

circuit assertSwapPreconditions(
  account: Bytes<32>,
  colA: Bytes<32>,
  val: Uint<128>,
  coinB: ShieldedCoinInfo,
  credit: Bytes<32>
): Bytes<32> {
  // 0. parameter sanity.
  assert(val > 0, "swap must give a positive amount");
  assert(coinB.value > 0, "swap must want a positive amount");
  assert(colA != coinB.color, "swap legs must be different colours");
  // 1. authorization already derived and checked the account before dispatch.
  const acct = account;
  // 2. THE PER-ACCOUNT, PER-COLOUR GUARD, BEFORE any pool guard.
  assert(shieldedBalanceOf(acct, colA) >= val, "account colour balance too low");
  // 3. only now, the pool.
  assert(pools.member(colA), "no pooled coin for this colour");
  assert(pools.lookup(colA).value >= val, "pooled colour balance too low");
  // 4. the credit target.
  assert(accounts.member(credit), "credit account is not registered");
  return acct;
}

/// The WANT leg, shared by both shapes: claim `coinB` into custody and attribute it to `credit`.
///
/// `receiveShielded` creates an output the transaction does not fund, so this is what makes the offer
/// UNBALANCED — a deficit of `coinB.value` of `coinB.color` at the guaranteed segment. It is the same
/// deficit `depositShielded` creates, which is why spike S1 could prove the taker mechanics against
/// v3 before v4 existed.
circuit claimWantedColour(coinB: ShieldedCoinInfo, credit: Bytes<32>): [] {
  // Must precede insertCoin: this is what allocates the Merkle-tree index.
  receiveShielded(coinB);
  if (pools.member(coinB.color)) {
    pools.insertCoin(
      coinB.color,
      mergeCoinImmediate(pools.lookup(coinB.color), coinB),
      right<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>(kernel.self())
    );
  } else {
    pools.insertCoin(coinB.color, coinB, right<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>(kernel.self()));
  }
  shieldedBalances.insert(
    shieldedKey(credit, coinB.color),
    (shieldedBalanceOf(credit, coinB.color) + coinB.value) as Uint<128>
  );
}

/// Write back what is left of colour `col`'s pool after a debit, or drop the colour entirely.
circuit repoolOrRemove(col: Bytes<32>, change: Maybe<ShieldedCoinInfo>): [] {
  if (change.is_some) {
    pools.insertCoin(col, change.value, right<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>(kernel.self()));
  } else {
    // Fully spent: the colour leaves the map entirely.
    pools.remove(col);
  }
}

// --- FR-308 — the swap maker, both shapes in ONE circuit ---------------------------------------

/// Give `valA` of `colourA` and require `coinB` into custody, atomically, in ONE circuit.
///
/// `recipientA` selects the FR-308 shape, and the two branches differ in exactly one respect — whether
/// an OUTPUT is created for the value that leaves custody:
///
///   some(key)   `sendShielded` pays `valA` to that key and re-pools the change, so the A leg is
///               internally balanced and the transaction's ONLY imbalance is the −`coinB.value`
///               deficit of `coinB.color`. A taker holding B funds that deficit, and cannot obtain A
///               without doing so, because both legs live in the same call.
///
///               The recipient is fixed HERE, at proving time, because a contract-sent shielded coin's
///               recipient enters the claimed commitment inside the proof. A contract simply cannot
///               `sendShielded` to "whoever takes this offer" — which is why this branch is the NAMED
///               taker (FR-308 v1), and why the bearer-key shape (v2b) has to name a THROWAWAY key and
///               publish its secret rather than leave the recipient open.
///
///   none        the pooled coin is consumed as a zswap input and its nullifier is claimed, but the
///               ONLY output created is the change back to this contract. The released `valA` is left
///               as a genuine POSITIVE imbalance at the guaranteed segment, addressed to nobody.
///               Surplus is legal in zswap balancing where a deficit is fatal, so the offer stays
///               valid and any taker's STOCK balancer sweeps the surplus into an output of its own
///               while funding the −B deficit. No recipient is fixed at proving time at all, so a
///               holder whose keys the maker never knew can take it (FR-308 v2a, owner-REQUIRED).
///
/// WHY THE `none` BRANCH IS LEGAL AND NOT A TRICK, read from the pinned ledger: contract-associated
/// nullifiers must EXACTLY equal the claimed nullifiers and contract-associated output commitments
/// must EXACTLY equal the claimed receives (`ledger/src/verify.rs:1528` and `:1548`) — both hold — while
/// claimed SPENDS need only be a SUBSET of the transaction's commitments (`:1599`). Claiming no spend
/// for a value that has no output is therefore within the rules: the value is simply unassigned, and
/// the zswap balance equation is what accounts for it.
///
/// Returns nothing. v3's `withdrawShielded` hands back the coin it sent, but there is no useful
/// analogue here: the `none` branch pays out no coin at all, and the maker already knows the terms it
/// asked for — the artifact, not a return value, is what a taker reads.
circuit openSwapShielded(
  account: Bytes<32>,
  colourA: Bytes<32>,
  valA: Uint<128>,
  recipientA: Maybe<Either<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>>,
  coinB: ShieldedCoinInfo,
  creditAccount: Bytes<32>
): [] {
  // The offer's terms are public facts of the transaction — the pooled coin, the payout and the
  // credited balance all land in ledger state — so they are disclosed once, up front.
  const colA = disclose(colourA);
  const val = disclose(valA);
  const rcpt = disclose(recipientA);
  const cb = disclose(coinB);
  const credit = disclose(creditAccount);

  const acct = assertSwapPreconditions(account, colA, val, cb, credit);
  const selfAddr = kernel.self();
  const pooled = pools.lookup(colA);

  if (rcpt.is_some) {
    // --- GIVE: −A, paid to the named recipient, change re-pooled. Internally balanced. ---
    const result = sendShielded(pooled, rcpt.value, val);
    repoolOrRemove(colA, result.change);
  } else {
    // --- GIVE: −A released to NOBODY, becoming a segment-0 SURPLUS ---
    createZswapInput(pooled);
    kernel.claimZswapNullifier(zswapCoinNullifier(dropMerkleIndex(pooled), selfAddr));
    const changeValue = (pooled.value - val) as Uint<128>;
    if (changeValue == 0) {
      repoolOrRemove(colA, none<ShieldedCoinInfo>());
    } else {
      // A fresh nonce for the change coin. `evolveNonce`'s domain differs from the one `sendShielded`
      // uses for its own change, and the pooled coin can be spent only once anyway (its nullifier is
      // consumed right above), so no commitment this contract ever creates can collide with it.
      const changeCoin = ShieldedCoinInfo{
        nonce: evolveNonce(2, pooled.nonce),
        color: colA,
        value: changeValue
      };
      const selfRecipient = right<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>(selfAddr);
      createZswapOutput(changeCoin, selfRecipient);
      const cm = zswapCoinCommitment(changeCoin, selfRecipient);
      kernel.claimZswapCoinSpend(cm);
      kernel.claimZswapCoinReceive(cm);
      repoolOrRemove(colA, some<ShieldedCoinInfo>(changeCoin));
    }
  }

  shieldedBalances.insert(shieldedKey(acct, colA), (shieldedBalanceOf(acct, colA) - val) as Uint<128>);

  // --- WANT: +B, credited to `creditAccount` ---
  claimWantedColour(cb, credit);
}

// ================================================================================================
// Manager v5 candidate — one dual-authorization registration/debit gateway
// ================================================================================================

circuit assertActionEnvelope(p: ExecutePayload): [] {
  assert(p.selector <= 6, "unknown execute selector");
  assert(p.authMode <= 1, "unknown authorization mode");

  if (p.selector == 0) {
    assert(p.authMode == 0, "native registration requires native authorization");
    assert(p.account == default<Bytes<32>>, "native registration account is derived");
    assert(p.owner == default<Bytes<20>> && p.accountSalt == default<Bytes<32>>, "native registration EVM fields must be inactive");
    assert(p.nonce == 0 && p.validUntil == 0, "native registration replay fields must be inactive");
    assert(p.primaryColor == default<Bytes<32>> && p.primaryAmount == 0, "native registration action fields must be inactive");
    assert(p.recipientKind == 0 && p.recipient == default<Bytes<32>>, "native registration recipient must be inactive");
    assert(p.toAccount == default<Bytes<32>> && p.wantNonce == default<Bytes<32>>, "native registration targets must be inactive");
    assert(p.wantColor == default<Bytes<32>> && p.wantAmount == 0 && p.creditAccount == default<Bytes<32>>, "native registration swap fields must be inactive");
    return;
  }

  if (p.selector == 1) {
    assert(p.authMode == 1, "EVM registration requires EVM authorization");
    assert(p.account != default<Bytes<32>>, "EVM registration account must be supplied");
    assert(p.owner != default<Bytes<20>>, "EVM registration owner must be nonzero");
    assert(p.accountSalt != default<Bytes<32>>, "EVM registration salt must be nonzero");
    assert(p.nonce == 0 && p.validUntil > 0, "EVM registration replay fields are noncanonical");
    assert(p.primaryColor == default<Bytes<32>> && p.primaryAmount == 0, "EVM registration action fields must be inactive");
    assert(p.recipientKind == 0 && p.recipient == default<Bytes<32>>, "EVM registration recipient must be inactive");
    assert(p.toAccount == default<Bytes<32>> && p.wantNonce == default<Bytes<32>>, "EVM registration targets must be inactive");
    assert(p.wantColor == default<Bytes<32>> && p.wantAmount == 0 && p.creditAccount == default<Bytes<32>>, "EVM registration swap fields must be inactive");
    return;
  }

  assert(p.account != default<Bytes<32>>, "action account must be supplied");
  assert(p.accountSalt == default<Bytes<32>>, "action account salt must be inactive");
  if (p.authMode == 0) {
    assert(p.owner == default<Bytes<20>>, "native action owner must be inactive");
    assert(p.nonce == 0 && p.validUntil == 0, "native action replay fields must be inactive");
  } else {
    assert(p.owner != default<Bytes<20>>, "EVM action owner must be nonzero");
    assert(p.validUntil > 0, "EVM action deadline must be nonzero");
  }

  if (p.selector == 2 || p.selector == 3) {
    assert(p.primaryAmount > 0, "withdraw amount must be positive");
    assert(p.recipientKind <= 1, "withdraw recipient kind is invalid");
    assert(p.recipient != default<Bytes<32>>, "withdraw recipient must be nonzero");
    assert(p.toAccount == default<Bytes<32>>, "withdraw transfer target must be inactive");
    assert(p.wantNonce == default<Bytes<32>> && p.wantColor == default<Bytes<32>>, "withdraw swap fields must be inactive");
    assert(p.wantAmount == 0 && p.creditAccount == default<Bytes<32>>, "withdraw swap target must be inactive");
    return;
  }

  if (p.selector == 4 || p.selector == 5) {
    assert(p.primaryAmount > 0, "internal transfer must be positive");
    assert(p.recipientKind == 0 && p.recipient == default<Bytes<32>>, "internal transfer recipient must be inactive");
    assert(p.toAccount != default<Bytes<32>>, "internal transfer target must be supplied");
    assert(p.wantNonce == default<Bytes<32>> && p.wantColor == default<Bytes<32>>, "internal transfer swap fields must be inactive");
    assert(p.wantAmount == 0 && p.creditAccount == default<Bytes<32>>, "internal transfer swap target must be inactive");
    return;
  }

  assert(p.selector == 6, "unknown execute selector");
  assert(p.primaryAmount > 0, "swap must give a positive amount");
  assert(p.recipientKind <= 2, "swap recipient kind is invalid");
  if (p.recipientKind == 0) {
    assert(p.recipient == default<Bytes<32>>, "open swap recipient must be zero");
  } else {
    assert(p.recipient != default<Bytes<32>>, "named swap recipient must be nonzero");
  }
  assert(p.toAccount == default<Bytes<32>>, "swap transfer target must be inactive");
  assert(p.wantAmount > 0, "swap must want a positive amount");
  assert(p.primaryColor != p.wantColor, "swap legs must be different colours");
  assert(p.creditAccount != default<Bytes<32>>, "swap credit account must be supplied");
}

circuit assertLiveDeadline(validUntil: Uint<64>): [] {
  assert(validUntil > 3600, "EVM authorization deadline cannot satisfy the horizon");
  const earliest = (validUntil - 3600) as Uint<64>;
  assert(blockTimeGte(earliest), "EVM authorization deadline exceeds 3600-second horizon");
  assert(blockTimeLt(validUntil), "EVM authorization has expired");
}

circuit authenticatedActionAccount(
  p: ExecutePayload,
  nativeAccount: Bytes<32>
): Bytes<32> {
  if (p.authMode == 0) {
    const acct = authenticatedNativeAccount(nativeAccount);
    assert(acct == p.account, "native witness does not match supplied account transcript");
    assert(accountModes.lookup(acct) == p.authMode, "authorization mode does not match account record");
    assert(!evmOwners.member(acct) && !evmNonces.member(acct), "native account carries EVM state");
    return acct;
  }

  assert(p.authMode == 1, "unknown account authorization mode");
  assert(accounts.member(p.account), "gateway account is not registered");
  assert(accountModes.member(p.account), "registered account has no authorization mode");
  assert(accountModes.lookup(p.account) == 1, "authorization mode does not match account record");
  assert(evmOwners.member(p.account) && evmNonces.member(p.account), "EVM account record is incomplete");
  assert(p.owner == evmOwners.lookup(p.account), "signed owner does not match stored owner");
  assert(p.nonce == evmNonces.lookup(p.account), "EVM nonce mismatch");
  return p.account;
}

circuit gatewayAccount(
  p: ExecutePayload,
  nativeAccount: Bytes<32>,
  evmRegistrationAccount: Bytes<32>
): Bytes<32> {
  if (p.selector == 0) {
    return nativeAccount;
  }
  if (p.selector == 1) {
    return evmRegistrationAccount;
  }
  return authenticatedActionAccount(p, nativeAccount);
}

circuit shieldedRecipient(kind: Uint<8>, recipient: Bytes<32>): Either<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress> {
  if (kind == 0) {
    return left<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>(ZswapCoinPublicKey{ bytes: recipient });
  }
  assert(kind == 1, "shielded recipient kind is invalid");
  return right<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>(ContractAddress{ bytes: recipient });
}

circuit unshieldedRecipient(kind: Uint<8>, recipient: Bytes<32>): Either<ContractAddress, UserAddress> {
  if (kind == 0) {
    return left<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(ContractAddress{ bytes: recipient });
  }
  assert(kind == 1, "unshielded recipient kind is invalid");
  return right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(UserAddress{ bytes: recipient });
}

circuit swapRecipient(
  kind: Uint<8>, recipient: Bytes<32>
): Maybe<Either<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>> {
  if (kind == 0) {
    return none<Either<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>>();
  }
  if (kind == 1) {
    return some<Either<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>>(
      left<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>(ZswapCoinPublicKey{ bytes: recipient })
    );
  }
  assert(kind == 2, "swap recipient kind is invalid");
  return some<Either<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>>(
    right<ZswapCoinPublicKey, ContractAddress>(ContractAddress{ bytes: recipient })
  );
}

// --- FR-031 authoritative semantic commitment -------------------------------------------------

circuit semanticTag(): Bytes<32> {
  return Bytes[0xe2, 0x11, 0x48, 0x48, 0x81, 0xf2, 0x54, 0x57,
               0xd3, 0x38, 0xef, 0xff, 0x10, 0x5e, 0x35, 0x53,
               0x31, 0x43, 0x51, 0x33, 0x8b, 0x55, 0xee, 0x21,
               0x55, 0x39, 0x10, 0x6f, 0xf5, 0xee, 0x9c, 0x6e];
}

circuit callTranscriptTag(): Bytes<32> {
  return Bytes[0x6b, 0x26, 0xfd, 0x1f, 0x96, 0x66, 0x83, 0x29,
               0x9a, 0xae, 0x5e, 0x9e, 0x5e, 0xcd, 0x2c, 0x57,
               0x5f, 0x1a, 0x17, 0xe9, 0x16, 0xdb, 0x0a, 0x6f,
               0xb2, 0x9d, 0xcd, 0x9e, 0xd8, 0x62, 0xf0, 0xdf];
}

circuit nativeAuthTag(): Bytes<32> {
  return Bytes[0x2a, 0xdb, 0xa8, 0x74, 0x39, 0x0d, 0xb7, 0x25,
               0xf4, 0x89, 0x15, 0x21, 0x7d, 0xa7, 0x03, 0xe3,
               0xfd, 0x62, 0xec, 0x40, 0x29, 0x6c, 0x2a, 0x2d,
               0x02, 0x60, 0x2e, 0x67, 0xa3, 0xb9, 0xe7, 0xf4];
}

circuit entrypointHash(): Bytes<32> {
  return Bytes[0xc6, 0x40, 0x06, 0x0c, 0xdb, 0x34, 0xfc, 0xc2,
               0x60, 0xf4, 0x1e, 0xac, 0x74, 0x74, 0xee, 0x1d,
               0x7c, 0x80, 0xb7, 0xe3, 0x60, 0x7d, 0xaf, 0xf9,
               0xac, 0x67, 0xc7, 0xea, 0x2e, 0xbb, 0x1c, 0x44];
}

circuit semanticEventName(): Bytes<32> {
  return Bytes[0x53, 0x5c, 0x10, 0x31, 0xf5, 0x85, 0xe2, 0xd7,
               0xa7, 0x95, 0xd0, 0xe3, 0x32, 0xa9, 0x74, 0x18,
               0xcd, 0x8e, 0xdd, 0xde, 0x40, 0xee, 0xfa, 0x21,
               0x4f, 0xb7, 0x8a, 0x2e, 0x18, 0x81, 0x2c, 0x1a];
}

circuit nativeAuthResult(account: Bytes<32>): Bytes<32> {
  const preimage: Bytes<64> = slice<64>([...nativeAuthTag(), ...account], 0) as Bytes<64>;
  return keccak256<Bytes<64>>(preimage);
}

circuit actionUnionHash(p: ExecutePayload): Bytes<32> {
  const preimage: Bytes<384> = slice<384>([
    ...addressWord(p.owner), ...uint64Word(p.validUntil), ...p.accountSalt,
    ...p.primaryColor, ...uint128Word(p.primaryAmount), ...uint8Word(p.recipientKind),
    ...p.recipient, ...p.toAccount, ...p.wantNonce, ...p.wantColor,
    ...uint128Word(p.wantAmount), ...p.creditAccount
  ], 0) as Bytes<384>;
  return keccak256<Bytes<384>>(preimage);
}

circuit semanticCallTranscriptHash(
  manager: Bytes<32>, p: ExecutePayload, account: Bytes<32>, authResult: Bytes<32>
): Bytes<32> {
  const preimage: Bytes<256> = slice<256>([
    ...callTranscriptTag(), ...manager, ...entrypointHash(), ...uint8Word(p.selector),
    ...frozenTypeHash(p.selector), ...account, ...authResult, ...actionUnionHash(p)
  ], 0) as Bytes<256>;
  return keccak256<Bytes<256>>(preimage);
}

circuit semanticCommitmentFromSlots(
  manager: Bytes<32>, domain: Bytes<32>, p: ExecutePayload, account: Bytes<32>,
  authResult: Bytes<32>, imbalanceCount: Uint<8>,
  family0: Uint<8>, color0: Bytes<32>, direction0: Uint<8>, amount0: Uint<128>,
  family1: Uint<8>, color1: Bytes<32>, direction1: Uint<8>, amount1: Uint<128>
): Bytes<32> {
  const semanticNonce = p.authMode == 1 ? p.nonce : (0 as Uint<64>);
  const callHash = semanticCallTranscriptHash(manager, p, account, authResult);
  const preimage: Bytes<1024> = slice<1024>([
    ...semanticTag(), ...manager, ...domain, ...entrypointHash(), ...uint8Word(p.selector),
    ...frozenTypeHash(p.selector), ...account, ...uint8Word(p.authMode), ...authResult,
    ...uint64Word(semanticNonce), ...addressWord(p.owner), ...uint64Word(p.validUntil),
    ...p.accountSalt, ...p.primaryColor, ...uint128Word(p.primaryAmount),
    ...uint8Word(p.recipientKind), ...p.recipient, ...p.toAccount, ...p.wantNonce,
    ...p.wantColor, ...uint128Word(p.wantAmount), ...p.creditAccount, ...callHash,
    ...uint8Word(imbalanceCount),
    ...uint8Word(family0), ...color0, ...uint8Word(direction0), ...uint128Word(amount0),
    ...uint8Word(family1), ...color1, ...uint8Word(direction1), ...uint128Word(amount1)
  ], 0) as Bytes<1024>;
  return keccak256<Bytes<1024>>(preimage);
}

/// Private slot-selection core used by both the exported byte oracle and the one execute event.
/// Muxing values into one call keeps every frozen byte while avoiding branch-duplicated Keccak.
circuit semanticCommitmentCore(
  manager: Bytes<32>, domain: Bytes<32>, payload: ExecutePayload,
  account: Bytes<32>, authResult: Bytes<32>
): Bytes<32> {
  const p = payload;
  const isSwap = p.selector == 6;
  const isOpenSwap = isSwap && p.recipientKind == 0;
  const giveBeforeWant = isOpenSwap && bytes32LexicographicLt(p.primaryColor, p.wantColor);
  const imbalanceCount = (isSwap ? (isOpenSwap ? 2 : 1) : 0) as Uint<8>;
  const family0 = (isSwap ? 1 : 0) as Uint<8>;
  const color0 = giveBeforeWant
    ? p.primaryColor
    : (isSwap ? p.wantColor : default<Bytes<32>>);
  const direction0 = (giveBeforeWant ? 2 : (isSwap ? 1 : 0)) as Uint<8>;
  const amount0 = (giveBeforeWant ? p.primaryAmount : (isSwap ? p.wantAmount : 0)) as Uint<128>;
  const family1 = (isOpenSwap ? 1 : 0) as Uint<8>;
  const color1 = isOpenSwap
    ? (giveBeforeWant ? p.wantColor : p.primaryColor)
    : default<Bytes<32>>;
  const direction1 = (isOpenSwap ? (giveBeforeWant ? 1 : 2) : 0) as Uint<8>;
  const amount1 = (isOpenSwap ? (giveBeforeWant ? p.wantAmount : p.primaryAmount) : 0) as Uint<128>;
  return semanticCommitmentFromSlots(
    manager, domain, p, account, authResult, imbalanceCount,
    family0, color0, direction0, amount0,
    family1, color1, direction1, amount1
  );
}

export circuit semanticCommitmentFor(
  manager: Bytes<32>, domain: Bytes<32>, payload: ExecutePayload,
  account: Bytes<32>, authResult: Bytes<32>
): Bytes<32> {
  const p = payload;
  assertActionEnvelope(p);
  return disclose(semanticCommitmentCore(manager, domain, p, account, authResult));
}

circuit emitSemanticCommitment(
  manager: Bytes<32>, p: ExecutePayload, account: Bytes<32>, authResult: Bytes<32>
): [] {
  const commitment = disclose(semanticCommitmentCore(manager, deploymentDomain, p, account, authResult));
  // `Misc.payload` is fixed at Bytes<256>. Put the authoritative commitment first; every committed
  // field is recovered from the proved execute transcript and recomputed before this value is
  // accepted. The pinned simulator currently exposes only the first 192 bytes of the 288-byte raw
  // event cell, so readers deliberately support its degraded raw-prefix representation too.
  const eventPayload: Bytes<256> = slice<256>([
    ...commitment, ...default<Bytes<32>>, ...default<Bytes<32>>, ...default<Bytes<32>>,
    ...default<Bytes<32>>, ...default<Bytes<32>>, ...default<Bytes<32>>, ...default<Bytes<32>>
  ], 0) as Bytes<256>;
  emit(Misc { name: semanticEventName(), payload: eventPayload });
}

/// The only externally callable registration/debit gateway. ECDSA/address operations stay
/// straight-line because the pinned ZKIR-v3 backend cannot lower guarded secp operations.
export circuit execute(
  payload: ExecutePayload,
  sig: Secp256k1EcdsaSignature,
  pk: Secp256k1Point
): [] {
  const p = disclose(payload);
  assertActionEnvelope(p);
  const manager = kernel.self().bytes;
  const digest = p.selector == 0
    ? default<Bytes<32>>
    : evmDigestFor(manager, deploymentDomain, p);
  const signatureOk = disclose(secp256k1EcdsaVerify(digest, sig, pk));
  const signer = disclose(secp256k1EthereumAddress(pk));
  const isNativeRegistration = p.selector == 0;
  const isEvmRegistration = p.selector == 1;
  const isRegistration = isNativeRegistration || isEvmRegistration;
  const isEvmAuthorized = p.authMode == 1;
  const isEvmAction = isEvmAuthorized && !isEvmRegistration;
  const nativeAccount = ownerCommitment(localOwnerSecret());
  const evmRegistrationAccount = evmAccountIdFor(manager, p.owner, p.accountSalt);
  assert(!isEvmRegistration || evmRegistrationAccount == p.account, "EVM registration account id mismatch");
  const account = gatewayAccount(p, nativeAccount, evmRegistrationAccount);

  if (isEvmAuthorized) {
    assertLiveDeadline(p.validUntil);
  }
  assert(!isEvmRegistration || signatureOk, "EVM registration signature does not verify");
  assert(!isEvmRegistration || signer == p.owner, "EVM registration signer does not match owner");
  assert(!isEvmAction || signatureOk, "EVM signature does not verify");
  assert(!isEvmAction || signer == p.owner, "EVM signer does not control account");

  if (isRegistration) {
    registerAccount(account, (isEvmRegistration ? 1 : 0) as Uint<8>);
  }
  if (isEvmRegistration) {
    evmOwners.insert(account, p.owner);
  }

  if (!isRegistration) {
    if (p.selector == 2) {
      withdrawShielded(account, p.primaryColor, p.primaryAmount, shieldedRecipient(p.recipientKind, p.recipient));
    } else if (p.selector == 3) {
      withdrawUnshielded(account, p.primaryColor, p.primaryAmount, unshieldedRecipient(p.recipientKind, p.recipient));
    } else if (p.selector == 4) {
      transferInternalShielded(account, p.toAccount, p.primaryColor, p.primaryAmount);
    } else if (p.selector == 5) {
      transferInternalUnshielded(account, p.toAccount, p.primaryColor, p.primaryAmount);
    } else {
      openSwapShielded(
        account,
        p.primaryColor,
        p.primaryAmount,
        swapRecipient(p.recipientKind, p.recipient),
        ShieldedCoinInfo{ nonce: p.wantNonce, color: p.wantColor, value: p.wantAmount },
        p.creditAccount
      );
    }
  }

  // The sole checked nonce write is deliberately after custody dispatch: every guard/effect must
  // succeed first. EVM registration selects zero; native records never enter this write.
  const incrementedNonce = (p.nonce + 1) as Uint<64>;
  assert(!isEvmAction || incrementedNonce > p.nonce, "EVM nonce overflow");
  const storedNonce = (isEvmRegistration ? 0 : incrementedNonce) as Uint<64>;
  if (isEvmAuthorized) {
    evmNonces.insert(account, storedNonce);
  }

  const authResult = isEvmAuthorized ? digest : nativeAuthResult(account);
  emitSemanticCommitment(manager, p, account, authResult);
}
